{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794442198,"nonce":"zHtKC8qBCIvCOt8CfTY1jKq6xPQ=","message":"Progress, 2026-11-12. Two commits, local only (owner: no push until real visual tests). Neither changes what any existing shader draws.\n\nDone\n- The slot lane (bb203f0f): GpuPrimitive gains node_slot (560 bytes), GpuGlyph carries it in flags[2], the shader struct and the tier-2 loader read it. Output unchanged: the recorder goldens (300), the layout and cn suites, the demo captures and the 59 GPU scene tests in blinc_test_suite pass. Three harness tests in blinc_test_suite fail with or without this work (they assert a 400x300 default the harness no longer has).\n- The table in the shader (7105f773): `NodeRow` (motion, pivot, link: three vec4s) and `apply_node` in `sdf_russl/shared/node_table.rs`; `prim()` in access.rs composes a node's delta onto the stored primitive, then each parent's, up to four, when the shader is built with `NODES = 1`. Default 0, so every existing shader is the same text. `sdf_core_nodes` is the core shader built with it (test-only for now). Tier 0 (storage buffer) and tier 2 (three-texel data-texture rows) are both written; tier 0 is what is tested. The HLSL guard covers the new variant.\n- A parity test (`node_parity.rs`) renders each delta twice: primitives patched on the CPU the way `apply_binding_deltas` patches them, drawn by the ordinary core shader, against the unpatched primitives and a node row, drawn by the table shader. Translate, scale (two cases), rotate (two), opacity: worst channel difference 0 or 1, no pixel over two levels. Combined and parent-chain cases are checked against the CSS composition. `BLINC_NODE_PARITY_OUT=\u003cdir\u003e` writes each case as a PNG (CPU frame, table frame, difference x32); I looked at them, and they show what they should: shapes orbiting the pivot with their borders turning, a fade through the checkerboard, a parent's move and fade over a child's scale and turn.\n\nFindings that decide later steps\n- The patcher applies each change to what the last one left, with the pivot fixed at the node's rest centre, so a translation that happens together with a scale is itself scaled, by the ratio of that frame. That makes combined motion frame-rate dependent. The table gives the absolute CSS composition (scale, turn, then translate about the pivot). They agree exactly for one property at a time, which is what the parity cases check, and differ for combined motion, where the table is right. Worth a visual look on a real scene before it goes live.\n- The patcher leaves the border width, the shadow offset and blur, the clip and the gradient geometry where they were. The table matches that for now (it is parity), so a scaled card keeps a 1x border. Scaling them is a one-line change in `apply_node` once the owner wants the visual difference; it is the CSS behaviour.\n- Fill alpha: the table also fades the gradient end and the side colours, which the patcher does not.\n\nNot done\n- Tier 1 and tier 2 vertex stages read instance fields from a vertex buffer, not the table, so they need the composition in the vertex stage (tier 2 can fetch the data texture there; tier 1 keeps the CPU patcher, per the plan).\n- The renderer: a NodeTable resource, binding 6 in the production bind group layouts and every site that builds those bind groups, the text, image, svg and path pipelines, glass, the shadow, 3D and notch pipelines (only core is switched).\n- The producer: blinc_layout's animation tracks writing rows instead of patching the batch, and the compositor paths that bail to a full walk for text.\n- Scene-level visual tests: the same scenes through the CPU-patched path and the table path in the recorder, compared and looked at, before any flag turns on. That is the test that gates a push.","files":null}]}